# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Line endings.
#
# This matters more here than in most projects, because tools/*.sh does not run
# on the machine that edits it. Home Assistant OS runs BusyBox ash, which does
# NOT tolerate carriage returns: a blank CRLF line becomes the command `\r`
# ("not found"), and `do\r` stops being the `do` keyword, so a for-loop dies
# with `syntax error: unexpected word (expecting "do")`.
#
# The trap is that this is invisible from Windows. Git Bash's bash strips the
# CR and runs a CRLF script quite happily, so rehearsing a deploy locally
# passes and the same file fails the moment it is scp'd to the box.
#
# Hence: everything that crosses to the box, or gets parsed there, is pinned to
# LF in the working tree — not merely normalised in the repository.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Default: normalise on commit, leave the working tree to the platform.
* text=auto

# Anything that runs on, or is read by, Home Assistant OS.
*.sh        text eol=lf
*.yaml      text eol=lf
*.yml       text eol=lf

# The local tooling, for the same reason plus consistency.
*.py        text eol=lf
*.md        text eol=lf
*.kt        text eol=lf
*.kts       text eol=lf
*.toml      text eol=lf
*.pro       text eol=lf
*.json      text eol=lf
*.xml       text eol=lf

# Gradle ships one of each, and both are load-bearing: the wrapper script runs
# under sh on Linux/macOS, the .bat under cmd.exe.
gradlew     text eol=lf
*.bat       text eol=crlf

# Never touched.
*.jar       binary
*.png       binary
*.apk       binary
